SECTION 3: USING UV
CREATE PROJECTS THE MODERN WAY
============================================================

uv makes it easy to create Python projects, manage dependencies, maintain consistent environments, and run code.

Let's build a real project using uv.

============================================================
1. CREATING A NEW PROJECT
============================================================

Create a new project and move into its directory:

Create a new project:
    uv init ai-assistant

Enter the project directory:
    cd ai-assistant

This creates a project structure similar to the following:

ai-assistant/
    .gitignore
    .python-version
    pyproject.toml
    README.md
    main.py

File descriptions:

.gitignore       - Files Git should ignore.
.python-version  - Python version for the project.
pyproject.toml   - Project configuration.
README.md        - Project documentation.
main.py          - Example Python script.

NOTE:

The .venv directory and uv.lock file are generally created when uv first needs to resolve and install the project's dependencies, such as when you run uv add, uv sync, or uv run.

============================================================
2. UNDERSTANDING pyproject.toml
============================================================

The pyproject.toml file is the central configuration file for a modern Python project.

A newly initialized project may contain something like this:

[project]
name = "ai-assistant"
version = "0.1.0"
description = "Add your description here"
readme = "README.md"
requires-python = ">=3.12"
dependencies = []

It stores important project information, including:

- Project name and version
- Python version requirements
- Project description
- Runtime dependencies
- Development tools and other configuration

In many modern projects, pyproject.toml brings together configuration that might previously have been spread across files such as:

requirements.txt - Commonly used to list installable dependencies.
setup.py         - Traditionally used for package configuration and installation.
setup.cfg        - Used for package and tool configuration.

Think of pyproject.toml as the central configuration file for your Python project.

It does not make every older configuration file unnecessary in every project, but it provides a modern, standardized place for project metadata and tool configuration.

============================================================
3. ADDING PACKAGES
============================================================

Use uv add to add dependencies to your project.

Add one package:
    uv add requests

Add multiple packages:
    uv add pandas numpy matplotlib

Add development dependencies:
    uv add --dev pytest black

When you add packages, uv automatically:

1. Updates pyproject.toml.
2. Resolves compatible package versions.
3. Updates or creates uv.lock.
4. Synchronizes the project's virtual environment.

Your pyproject.toml will contain configuration similar to this:

[project]
name = "ai-assistant"
version = "0.1.0"
description = "Add your description here"
readme = "README.md"
requires-python = ">=3.12"
dependencies = [
    "requests>=2.32.0",
    "pandas>=2.2.0",
    "numpy>=1.26.0",
    "matplotlib>=3.8.0",
]

[dependency-groups]
dev = [
    "pytest>=8.0.0",
    "black>=24.0.0",
]

Runtime dependencies are required by your application.

Development dependencies are used during development, testing, and code quality checks. They are not necessarily required by users running your application.

The exact version constraints depend on the packages available when you add them.

============================================================
4. UNDERSTANDING THE LOCK FILE
============================================================

One of the most useful features of uv is its lock file: uv.lock.

This file records the exact dependency resolution for your project, including package versions and their dependencies.

A simplified example:

version = 1
requires-python = ">=3.12"

[[package]]
name = "requests"
version = "2.32.3"
dependencies = [
    { name = "certifi" },
    { name = "charset-normalizer" },
]

The actual lock file contains more information than this simplified example.

WHY IS uv.lock IMPORTANT?

Imagine you and another developer are working on the same project.

Without a lock file, each developer might install different compatible versions of a dependency.

With uv.lock, both developers can install the same resolved dependency versions, helping maintain consistent environments.

Commit uv.lock to version control so other developers and deployment environments can use the same dependency resolution.

============================================================
5. RUNNING YOUR PYTHON CODE
============================================================

There are several ways to run Python code in a project managed by uv.

METHOD 1: USE uv run (RECOMMENDED)

    uv run python main.py

This is the simplest approach.

uv run automatically uses the project's environment and ensures its dependencies are synchronized when needed.

You do not need to activate the virtual environment manually.

METHOD 2: ACTIVATE THE VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENT

If you prefer the traditional approach, activate the project's virtual environment.

macOS/Linux:

    source .venv/bin/activate

Windows PowerShell:

    .venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1

Then run:

    python main.py

METHOD 3: USE THE PYTHON EXECUTABLE DIRECTLY

You can also run Python using the executable inside .venv.

macOS/Linux:

    .venv/bin/python main.py

Windows:

    .venv\Scripts\python.exe main.py

RECOMMENDATION:

Use uv run for everyday project development. It helps ensure that commands run with the correct project environment.

============================================================
6. COMMON uv COMMANDS
============================================================

Here are some useful commands to remember.

Create a new project:
    uv init project-name

Add a package:
    uv add package-name

Remove a package:
    uv remove package-name

Install and synchronize dependencies:
    uv sync

Upgrade a package:
    uv add --upgrade package-name

List installed packages:
    uv pip list

Run a Python script:
    uv run python script.py

Check the uv version:
    uv --version

These commands cover many common project management tasks.

============================================================
7. WORKING WITH EXISTING PROJECTS
============================================================

Already have a Python project using requirements.txt?

You can work with it using uv without starting from scratch.

OPTION 1: ADD REQUIREMENTS TO pyproject.toml

    uv add -r requirements.txt

This adds the listed requirements as project dependencies and updates the project configuration and lock file.

OPTION 2: INSTALL DIRECTLY FROM requirements.txt

    uv pip install -r requirements.txt

This uses uv's pip-compatible interface to install packages into the selected Python environment.

The second approach is useful when you want to keep using an existing requirements.txt workflow.

============================================================
8. UNDERSTANDING VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENTS
============================================================

uv manages project virtual environments automatically.

A few important points:

- The default project environment is stored in .venv.
- It is created when uv needs to synchronize project dependencies.
- You can use uv run without manually activating it.
- VS Code can use the .venv interpreter once it is selected or detected.
- The generated .gitignore commonly excludes .venv.

WHY USE A VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENT?

A virtual environment keeps each project's dependencies separate.

For example, one project can use an older version of a package while another project uses a newer version without interfering with each other.

============================================================
9. TIPS AND TRICKS
============================================================

A. INSTALL GLOBAL COMMAND-LINE TOOLS

Use uv tool install to install Python command-line tools in isolated environments.

    uv tool install black
    uv tool install mypy

This is useful for tools you want to run across multiple projects without adding them as dependencies to every project.

B. MANAGE PYTHON VERSIONS

uv can download and manage Python versions.

Install Python 3.12:
    uv python install 3.12

Install Python 3.11:
    uv python install 3.11

List available Python versions:
    uv python list

You can then configure a project's Python version using its .python-version file or other supported project settings.

C. DEFINE CUSTOM PROJECT COMMANDS

You can define command-line entry points in pyproject.toml using [project.scripts].

For example:

[project.scripts]
start = "ai_assistant.main:run"

This example assumes your project contains an importable ai_assistant package, a main.py module inside it, and a callable function named run.

After installing or synchronizing the project, you can run the command:

    uv run start

This is useful for creating convenient commands to launch your application.

